High atop a jagged peak, an ancient mathematician named Calder 
carved a cryptic message into the stone, a riddle to guard the 
path to triumph. Obsessed with the symmetry of his mountain, Calder 
devised a cipher that twisted words through numerical slopes. His final 
note, found etched beside the message, revealed his key: a grid of 
numbers etched as 6 9 atop 9 9, a square he called "the ridge’s lock."

The ciphertext, weathered but intact, reads:

ZOCC{Hnayv_Yav_Kfjq_Gl_Wkr}E

Legends claim Calder’s code transforms pairs of letters, sliding 
them along the mountain’s twin inclines—two numbers atop, 
two below. To unlock his secret, you must ascend this mathematical 
ridge, perhaps inverting its steep angles to descend back to meaning. 
The flag lies within, a testament to those who conquer the peak. 
